    "localmaxxing": {
      "backend": null,
      "hardware_label": "RTX 3080",
      "notes": "MTP arm, +56.5% over the spec-off row (47.32 -> 74.07). The MTP head survives abliteration in this build and delivers essentially the same speedup as the unmodified quant (+58.3%), so you do not lose speculative decoding by going abliterated - PROVIDED the build kept the nextn tensors. ANOMALY DISCLOSED: llama-server reports draft acceptance 0.796 (156/196, mean len 2.59) for this model, which is identical to five decimal places to what the unmodified unsloth quant reports on the same prompt, even though I verified the two models generate materially different text. I cannot explain the coincidence and am reporting the number with that caveat rather than as a validated cross-model comparison; treat the throughput figures, which are medians of 3 tight reps, as the reliable part. Headless 3080 (displays on the 7900X iGPU). Non-repetitive 2700-token synthetic log-summarisation prompt, ignore_eos forcing exactly 256 output tokens so token count is identical across models, temp 0, cache_prompt off, medians of 3 reps. llama.cpp b10472 (60eeeb608), Windows 11 / CUDA 12.4, PCIe 4.0 x16, stock 370 W. File verified byte-exact against HF (8,884,504,160 B) and confirmed to retain the MTP head (65 blocks, 866 tensors, 4 nextn tensors) - many abliterated Qwen3.8 GGUFs do NOT: one build in a repo literally named -MTP-GGUF has 64 blocks and zero nextn tensors, i.e. the MTP block was dropped entirely. Check the tensor list, not the repo name.",
